# Troubleshooting

## Power

- Check the common ground.
- Make sure the batteries are sufficiently charged.
- Check the battery voltage under load.
- Check the battery polarity.
- Make sure the battery can supply enough current.
- Check the Arduino's power supply.

## Wiring

- Check that all wires work properly.
- Check for loose breadboard connections.
- Check for short circuits.
- Verify the L298N power connections.
- Verify the IN1–IN4 connections.
- Check the ENA/ENB connections and jumpers.

## L298N

- Check the power jumper.
- Check for overheating.
- Check the L298N output voltage while the motors are running.

## Motors

- Make sure the motor terminals are properly soldered.
- Test each motor individually before installing it.
- Check motor polarity.
- Check that the motors can spin freely.
- Test the motors under mechanical load.

## Mechanical

- Make sure the wheels rotate freely.
- Check for excessive friction.
- Make sure the wheels are properly aligned.
- Make sure nothing is rubbing against the chassis.

## Software

- Verify the Arduino pin definitions.
- Verify the motor direction logic.
- Check the PWM values.
- Make sure the correct enable pins are being controlled.
